{
  "hostname": "BL-DT-0108",
  "reason": "Ticket sender's only registered desktop reports no network connectivity in the warehouse office; checking online state and warnings per KB-014."
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi James, I checked your account and your desktop (BL-DT-0108). Your account is active and not locked, but the desktop is showing as offline, so it has lost its connection to the network. I've passed this to our Tier 2 engineers with the details, since it needs hands-on checking and your shift starts soon. Please keep your desk phone or a phone handy in case they need to reach you, and if the office network seems down for others too, let us know right away.",
  "internal_note": "Sender: James O'Connor (user_id 8), Warehouse Supervisor, Operations. Account active, sign_in_blocked=false, failed_login_count=0, password not expired, VPN access enabled. Triage marked user_blocked=true, but the account lookup does not support that, so treat the triage flag as inaccurate. Device BL-DT-0108 (Windows 11 Pro, desktop, assigned to user): online=false, last check-in 2026-10-11 01:13 UTC, disk 78% free, no warnings. Desktop is offline, which confirms no network path, but KB-014 is written for Wi-Fi laptops and its fix steps (Wi-Fi toggle, router restart) do not map cleanly to a wired desktop. I could not confirm whether other office users are affected, which is the KB-014 escalation trigger, so I have not classified it as an office-wide outage. Escalating to Tier 2 for physical/network-port/switch-level check at the warehouse office and to confirm whether the office network is down for others. Priority P2, but the user's shift starts in about an hour, so Tier 2 should prioritise it. No password or unlock actions were taken.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-014"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
